                    briefing_text_r3:       `A tier structure only works if customers cannot cheaply grab the low tier while enjoying premium value. Design <strong>segmentation fences</strong> — and test each on two axes: <em>does it hold</em> (can it be gamed?) and <em>is it perceived as fair</em>? A verified student ID is a fence customers accept; their phone model is not. Fences built on hidden personal data destroy fairness — the very mistake that triggered the backlash. Choose your criterion, your transactional fence, and how hard you stress-test for arbitrage.`,
                    briefing_title_r4:      `Round 4 — Defend fairness & launch`,
                    briefing_text_r4:       `Launch into the live backlash. Craft the <strong>fairness narrative</strong>: how the new tiers differ from the leaked personalised pricing (same price for the same tier, criteria customers control). Decide the <strong>transition</strong> for 640,000 loyal subscribers — grandfather them, or migrate everyone overnight and watch a three-year commuter's price jump €14. Position against the entrant's €35 plan <em>without a margin-destroying price war</em>, and invest in transparent communication that pre-empts the authority. Silence reads as a cover-up; loyalty perceived as punished is the fastest route to churn.`,

