                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_power:         `<strong>Right — the power users are where the money sits.</strong> Multimodal heavy users extract far more value than €42 and would pay more, yet the flat plan charges them the same as a student. That uncaptured WTP at the top is exactly what a top tier monetises.`,
                    coach_r1_students:      `<strong>Careful — students are over-priced, not under-priced.</strong> They under-use €42 and are the ones drifting to the €35 entrant. The under-pricing is at the <em>top</em>, with the power users. Fix the diagnosis or you'll cut the wrong tier.`,
                    coach_r1_commuters:     `<strong>Commuters are roughly where they should be.</strong> They get steady mid value for €42. The flat plan's real distortion is at the extremes — over-charging students, under-charging power users. Look to the top for the uncaptured margin.`,
                    coach_r1_families:      `<strong>Families are a bundling opportunity, but not the biggest leak.</strong> The clearest under-pricing under one price is the high-WTP power user paying the same €42 as everyone else. Anchor there first.`,
                    coach_r1_diag_sharp:    `<strong>Sharp read.</strong> You located the uncaptured WTP at the top AND chose to anchor on value the customer self-selects — second-degree differentiation, the defensible route. That is Winkelmann's point: capture the margin through a logic the customer accepts.`,
                    coach_r1_personalised:  `<strong>That is exactly the practice that triggered #BrückeAbzocke.</strong> Device, location and browsing signals are first-degree personalised pricing — the same plan at a different price on an opaque criterion. The board halted it and the authority is investigating. Anchor on value, not on data the customer can't see.`,
                    coach_r1_discountall:   `<strong>A blanket discount throws away margin and your reference price.</strong> Cutting the headline for everyone is the opposite of capturing value — it trains the whole base to expect the floor and does nothing about the under-priced top.`,
                    coach_r1_cost:          `<strong>Cost-plus ignores the only thing that matters here — willingness-to-pay.</strong> Your segments value the service wildly differently; pricing on cost alone leaves money at the top and prices out the bottom, exactly the flat-plan problem you're trying to fix.`,
                    coach_r1_norigor:       `<strong>You're designing tiers without quantifying WTP or elasticity.</strong> Without segment-level WTP bands you can't set gaps or fences that hold — you'll guess the prices and the migration. Do the analysis before you draw the ladder.`,
                    coach_r2_gbb:           `<strong>Good-better-best is the right architecture.</strong> Three value-versioned tiers let customers self-select into the tier matching their WTP — you serve the price-sensitive and the premium at once without negotiating a single individual price.`,
                    coach_r2_flat:          `<strong>One flat plan is what left the money on the table.</strong> Adjusting a single price can't capture the spread of WTP across four very different segments — it's the problem, not the fix.`,
                    coach_r2_pricewar:      `<strong>A blanket €33 entry tier is a price war, not a tier.</strong> You'll win the students and torch your reference price across the whole base — loyal €42 customers will ask why they pay more for the same thing. The lower tier must be fenced and value-versioned, not a blanket cut.`,
                    coach_r2_manytiers:     `<strong>Seven micro-tiers is over-segmentation.</strong> Customers can't self-select cleanly through a confusing menu, the fences multiply, and cannibalisation risk climbs. Good-better-best captures most of the value with far less complexity.`,
                    coach_r2_entrylow:      `<strong>An entry price below €34 starts the price war.</strong> Undercutting the €35 entrant with a blanket low price erodes your reference price and invites the whole base to trade down. Fence a value tier just above the entrant instead.`,
                    coach_r2_entryhigh:     `<strong>Your entry tier is priced into the entrant's hands.</strong> Set it too high and the price-sensitive students you most need to retain simply leave for €35. A value entry tier has to be competitive — fenced, but competitive.`,
                    coach_r2_toplow:        `<strong>Your top tier leaves WTP uncaptured.</strong> The power users would pay well above this — pricing the Best tier too low repeats the flat-plan mistake at the top and forgoes the margin the whole exercise is meant to capture.`,
                    coach_r2_tophigh:       `<strong>A top tier above €70 reads as price-gouging.</strong> You may hit the margin on the spreadsheet, but the consumer group will call it exploitation and your fairness score won't recover. Capture WTP without crossing the line into a price you can't defend in public.`,
                    coach_r2_gapgood:       `<strong>Strong ladder.</strong> A good-better-best structure with disciplined gaps and well-placed prices captures value at the top while keeping mid and entry users in the right tier. That's self-selection working.`,
                    coach_r2_gaplow:        `<strong>Your gaps are too small — power users will buy down.</strong> If Best is barely above Better with most premium inclusions thrown into Better too, why would a power user ever pay for the top? They trade down, blended margin collapses, and you've trained your best customers to pay less.`,
                    coach_r3_controllable:  `<strong>Fair, controllable fences.</strong> Verified student status, commitment length, off-peak and household eligibility rest on reasons customers accept and choices they control — they hold against arbitrage and survive a journalist's screenshot. This is the opposite of the leaked practice.`,
                    coach_r3_data:          `<strong>This is the leaked practice, again.</strong> Gating the discount on device, location and browsing is first-degree personalised pricing — same plan, different price, opaque criterion. It is precisely what triggered the inquiry. Which of these fences would survive a screenshot?`,
                    coach_r3_nofence:       `<strong>No fence means the ladder leaks.</strong> If anyone can grab the cheap tier while enjoying premium value, your power users buy down and the structure collapses. A tier without a fence is just a discount everyone takes.`,
                    coach_r3_mixed:         `<strong>The quiet data-based fences are the problem.</strong> Mixing transparent criteria with a few hidden device/location signals still means the same plan at a different price on an opaque basis — one screenshot of the data-based one and you're back in the headlines.`,
                    coach_r3_lockin:        `<strong>A hard 24-month lock-in holds, but it reads as a trap.</strong> Steep exit fees feel like punishing customers rather than rewarding a controllable choice — fair fences earn the discount, they don't imprison the customer.`,
                    coach_r3_noarb:         `<strong>You haven't stress-tested the fences for gaming.</strong> A fence you didn't pressure-test is a leak waiting to happen — work out who games it and how before launch, or your power users find the loophole first.`,
                    coach_r4_transparent:   `<strong>Transparency is how trust recovers.</strong> Publicly retiring personalised pricing and explaining that everyone pays the same price for the same tier on criteria they control answers the authority and the press directly — fairness recovery is a communication act, not just a structural one.`,
                    coach_r4_silent:        `<strong>Silence reads as a cover-up.</strong> Your tiers are live, but you've said nothing about the personalised-pricing scandal that started this. The authority and the press want to know what changed and why it's different — your fairness score won't recover in the dark.`,
                    coach_r4_spin:          `<strong>Denying the leak makes it worse.</strong> Framing #BrückeAbzocke as a misunderstanding insults the customers who saw the screenshots and hardens the authority. The only route back is to own what happened and show what's genuinely different.`,
                    coach_r4_abrupt:        `<strong>An overnight migration punishes loyalty.</strong> A loyal three-year commuter who suddenly sees her price jump €14 with no transition is the fastest route to churn. Loyalty perceived as punished destroys the trust the whole launch is meant to rebuild.`,
                    coach_r4_valueversion:  `<strong>Fenced value response done right.</strong> You answered the €35 entrant with a fenced value tier and a transparent narrative — you differentiate on value rather than discounting until everyone expects the floor. That holds margin and reference price at once.`,
                    coach_r4_matchprice:    `<strong>Matching €35 across the base is a margin war.</strong> You stop a few students leaving and torch the contribution margin that funds the business — and you re-anchor every customer's reference price to the floor. Compete on value, not on a blanket cut.`,
                    coach_r4_ignore:        `<strong>Ignoring the entrant cedes the price-sensitive segment.</strong> The €35 plan is actively courting your students; assuming loyalty carries them is how you lose the volume that feeds the upper tiers. A fenced value tier answers them without a price war.`,

