                    insight_full:           `A fair and profitable ladder: you lifted blended margin past 39% and revenue past 8% while restoring the fairness score above 65 — all within budget. You captured the value through self-selection and fair fences, exactly the route that survives a journalist's screenshot and the authority's inquiry.`,
                    insight_marginonly:     `Margin won, trust lost: you hit the CFO's margin and revenue numbers but the fairness score never recovered above 65. A margin you can't defend in public won't survive contact with the authority — this is the classic margin-only design error.`,
                    insight_fairnotmargin:  `Fair but sub-target: customers accept the structure, but you left the CFO's margin target unmet — most likely by under-pricing the top tier or running a price war at the bottom. Fairness without the economics doesn't satisfy the board.`,
                    insight_short:          `You fell short of the targets. Re-examine the diagnosis: under-pricing sits at the top with the power users, the lower tier must be fenced not slashed, and fairness recovers through value-based fences plus a transparent narrative.`,
                    insight_cannib:         `Cannibalisation risk stayed high — your gaps and fences let power users buy down. A tier ladder only holds if the gap discipline and leak-proof fences keep each segment in the tier intended for it.`,
                    insight_lowfairness:    `The fairness score never cleared 65. Differentiation succeeds only when the customer perceives the logic as legitimate — data-based fences, gouging top prices or a silent launch all keep trust suppressed.`,
                    insight_overbudget:     `You overspent the €2.4M launch budget — most often by paying for personalised-pricing technology, hidden-data fences and an oversized campaign. A defensible structure shouldn't need to outspend the cap.`,
